      <h2>Shard: git-backed, graph-aware notes</h2>
      <p>Shard is a personal project: a self-hosted, Obsidian-style notes backend. Notes are plain markdown files with YAML frontmatter, stored in git repositories, and the graph of <code>[[wikilinks]]</code> between them is a first-class resource you can query through an API. The repo is private, so this page is the tour.</p>

      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>An API-first notes service: create, read, update and delete notes over HTTP, with optimistic concurrency so two editors cannot silently clobber each other.</li>
        <li>Content lives in git, not in a database. A vault is just a repository of markdown files, so it stays readable (and greppable) without Shard running.</li>
        <li>Links between notes are parsed out of the markdown and kept as a graph, so questions like "what links here?" are an API call instead of a full-text search.</li>
        <li>Several independent vaults can sit behind one API.</li>
      </ul>

